questions.a:
Encapsulation bundles data and behaviour together and protects invariants.
Key points: (1) Control access to data - an object's internal data should not normally be accessed or changed directly from outside the object. (2) Hide implementation details - other objects interact through the public methods/interface without needing to know how it works internally. (3) Protect valid behaviour/invariants.
C# example:
class BankAccount {
    private decimal balance;
    public void Deposit(decimal amount) { if (amount > 0) balance += amount; }
}
The balance cannot be set to an invalid value from outside.
